  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "JP Sears left after 3.2 innings and the offense never recovered from falling behind early.",
    "what_this_means": "The Padres' season rhythm came into sharp focus tonight: the pitching gives you a chance, and the bats have to finish it. Sears exited with damage already done, the Giants up 3-1 after two innings, and nothing in the Padres' lineup answered. They scored once on a runner already in motion. Luis Rengifo went 2-for-3 and one run crossed. But ten strikeouts silenced any momentum. The bullpen held. Wandy Peralta and Randy V\u00e1squez threw 5.1 clean innings after the starter left, giving the offense time to find something. It never did. They trailed the entire game and never led once. When you leave nine innings empty-handed at the plate while chasing, one run is not a strategy. Ten left on base, one converted. That's the math that breaks your streak.",
    "what_to_watch": "Carson Whisenhunt takes the mound for San Francisco tonight at 6:45 PT. Bradgley Rodriguez counters with a 0.00 ERA over his last three starts. The kind of clean arm the Padres need if the offense stays this quiet.",
